// @test: two closures over one binding do not share it (immutable snapshot vs write-back)
// @description: pins the hazard #384 names: a read-only closure over a local keeps its
//   snapshot, so a mutation performed through a second, mutable-capturing closure is NOT
//   visible through the first. The mutation does reach the enclosing frame. The boxed-capture
//   contract keeps this: a capture that only reads stays a snapshot, and only a capture that
//   assigns turns the binding into a shared heap cell. Fixture is the pre-change baseline.
// @category: lambda, closure, capture, mutable
// @expect-output: inc=1 get=0 local=1
// @expect-return: 0

main()<Int> -> {
    c<Int> = 0
    inc<fn() -> Int> = || -> { c = c + 1; return c }
    get<fn() -> Int> = || -> { return c }

    i1<Int> = inc()
    g1<Int> = get()
    l1<Int> = c
    println("inc=" + i1.to_string() + " get=" + g1.to_string() + " local=" + l1.to_string())
    return 0
}
